I think Slashem is much easier than Nethack, with the possible
exception of Lycanthropes.

Even the Vampire class is so powerful that I literally picked a Vampire
ice mage, ditched my pet, grabbed a +0 dagger, and easily retrieved the
luckstone by meleeing everything I found, without using Elbereth.

In the Gnomish Mines, they replaced all the random difficult monsters
(i.e. swarms of ants, mumaks) with baby dragons.  For the rest of the
game, everytime you see something new, just summon up the same monster
in wizard mode so you don't get surprised by it.  Without the element
of surprise, they aren't scary.

You aren't required to do the special Slashem levels right when you
find them.  Just go back to them after you are ready for them.

Having a special level for every unique demon makes the game easier.
The scary part was worrying that a unique demon might get randomly
summoned when you were dealing with other stuff.  Now you get to
overprepare for every unique demon, because you know exactly where they
all are.

The Techniques were the worst idea ever.  Most of them have such a high
timeout that I never use them, because I feel the need to save them "in
case of an emergency".  Turning the AoE effect of skilled+ fireball
(and cone of cold) into techniques was the second worst idea ever.  The
third worst idea ever was turning the Monk resistances into techniques.

Having said all this, I still play Slashem every once in a while.  I
like the game, but I just don't think it's remotely hard unless my
character is a Lycanthrope.


